     The most common reason for this is an attempt to load an
incompatible bones file.  Usually that's one leftover from an older
version of the program, where the installation instructions haven't
been followed properly.  However, it could also happen if you ftp'd
somebody elses bones file and their original configuration wasn't
sufficiently close to your own.

     If you had the checkpoint option enabled (which is the default),
you might be able to use the recover program to reconstruct a save file.
Whether or not that will work depends on how much of the attempted
level change took place prior to the crash...



